     "id": "millais-christ-in-the-house-of-his-parents",
     "title": "Christ in the House of His Parents (The Carpenter's Shop)",
     "artist": "John Everett Millais",
     "date": "1849–1850",
     "year": 1850,
     "created": "London, England",
     "location": "Tate Britain, London, UK",
     "medium": "Oil on canvas",
     "form": "Painting",
     "style": "Pre-Raphaelite",
     "summary": "In Joseph's cluttered workshop the boy Jesus has cut his palm on a nail, and as Mary kneels to comfort him a drop of blood falls onto his foot, a quiet foreshadowing of the Crucifixion. The young Pre-Raphaelite Millais filled an ordinary carpenter's shop with symbols, from the dove on the ladder to the young John the Baptist carrying a bowl of water, presenting the Holy Family with startling realism.",
     "facts": [
      "When shown at the Royal Academy in 1850, it was exhibited with a verse from Zechariah: \"What are these wounds in thine hands?\"",
      "Charles Dickens attacked it fiercely, describing the boy Jesus as a \"hideous, wry-necked, blubbering, red-headed boy\" and Mary as grotesquely ugly.",
      "To achieve accuracy, Millais painted in a real carpenter's workshop on Oxford Street and used a carpenter's body for Joseph's arms.",
      "The sheep in the background represent the future flock of Christ's followers."
     ],
     "scripture": "Zechariah 13:6",
